WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.871 --> 00:00:06.751
What's up Wisecrack, Jared again.

00:00:06.751 --> 00:00:12.151
Today, we’re diving into the film series
that’s equal parts heart and fart —Dreamworks’

00:00:12.160 --> 00:00:14.650
enduring fairy tale sendup, Shrek.

00:00:14.650 --> 00:00:18.630
The Shrek franchise is well-known for its
signature brand of humor, which parodies fairy

00:00:18.630 --> 00:00:23.181
tales by juxtaposing classic tropes of the
genre with modern-day language, attitudes,

00:00:23.181 --> 00:00:24.821
and pop culture references.

00:00:24.821 --> 00:00:29.141
When the films are working (and they don’t
always), they make us laugh by showing what

00:00:29.141 --> 00:00:34.441
happens when the way we think the world ought
to be crashes up against the way that it actually

00:00:34.441 --> 00:00:35.441
is.

00:00:35.441 --> 00:00:40.471
At their best, the films are a radical celebration
of diversity, love, and the chaotic messiness

00:00:40.471 --> 00:00:45.131
of real life overthrowing pretension, rigidity,
and prejudice.

00:00:45.131 --> 00:00:46.531
At their worst, well...

00:00:46.531 --> 00:00:49.601
But we’ll get to that later.

00:00:49.611 --> 00:00:53.291
Welcome to this Wisecrack Edition on The Shrek
movies: Deep or Dumb?

00:00:53.291 --> 00:00:57.611
As always, spoilers for an 18-year-old franchise
about a Scottish ogre and an annoying

00:00:57.611 --> 00:00:58.651
talking donkey.

00:01:00.246 --> 00:01:02.806
Alright guys first, a quick recap of the series-to-date.

00:01:02.806 --> 00:01:08.376
We meet Shrek, an ornery bachelor of an ogre,
enjoying his life of solitude and Smash Mouth.

00:01:08.376 --> 00:01:13.946
Shrek’s idyllic existence is turned upside-down
when one Lord Farquaad transforms his beloved

00:01:13.946 --> 00:01:17.436
swamp into a forced resettlement camp for
fairy tale creatures.

00:01:17.436 --> 00:01:19.896
“We were forced to come here!”

00:01:19.896 --> 00:01:20.896
“By who?”

00:01:20.896 --> 00:01:21.896
“Lord Farquaad.

00:01:21.896 --> 00:01:26.086
He hoofed und he poofed und he...signed an
eviction notice.”

00:01:26.086 --> 00:01:31.476
To get his land back, Shrek agrees to go on
a quest: He—and chatterbox sidekick Donkey—will

00:01:31.476 --> 00:01:35.856
rescue Princess Fionna from a dragon’s lair,
so that Farquaad can marry her and become

00:01:35.856 --> 00:01:36.966
a legitimate king.

00:01:36.966 --> 00:01:39.936
“What I mean is, you’re not a king yet.

00:01:39.936 --> 00:01:41.416
But you can become one!

00:01:41.416 --> 00:01:43.586
All you have to do is marry a princess.”

00:01:43.586 --> 00:01:48.076
On the way back to Duloc, Shrek and Fionna
inadvertently fall in love, and are eventually

00:01:48.076 --> 00:01:49.076
married.

00:01:49.076 --> 00:01:52.286
The rest of the films in the series follow
Shrek and Fionna through several stereotypical

00:01:52.286 --> 00:01:57.676
hallmarks of newlywed life: meeting the parents
in Shrek 2; preparing for parenthood in Shrek

00:01:57.676 --> 00:02:01.886
the Third; and adjusting to a more settled
lifestyle in Shrek Forever After.

00:02:01.886 --> 00:02:06.886
In the first film, main antagonist Lord Farquaad
is obsessed with the pursuit of “perfection.”

00:02:06.886 --> 00:02:08.516
“I’m not the monster here—you are!

00:02:08.516 --> 00:02:13.776
You and the rest of that fairy tale trash,
poisoning my perfect world...”

00:02:13.776 --> 00:02:15.966
But what is “perfection?”

00:02:15.966 --> 00:02:20.456
According to Farquaad, it is a clean, well-ordered
kingdom, populated by citizens mechanically

00:02:20.456 --> 00:02:22.716
going about the roles he has assigned them:

00:02:22.716 --> 00:02:30.106
”Don’t make waves, stay in line, and we’ll
get along fine, Duloc is the perfect place.”

00:02:30.106 --> 00:02:34.176
Even his desire to marry Fionna is driven
more by compulsive completionism more than

00:02:34.176 --> 00:02:35.986
any real political need.

00:02:35.986 --> 00:02:40.866
As lord of Duloc, it is clear that he already
wields absolute power over his subjects, to

00:02:40.866 --> 00:02:44.036
the point of establishing a cult of personality
around his image:

00:02:44.036 --> 00:02:49.576
But to be de facto ruler is not enough for
Farquaad; he insists upon ruling, as he puts

00:02:49.576 --> 00:02:52.486
it: “the most perfect kingdom of them all”

00:02:52.486 --> 00:02:57.076
His desire to marry Fionna comes not from
love, but from a need to satisfy the Magic

00:02:57.076 --> 00:02:58.686
Mirror’s definition of kingship -

00:02:58.686 --> 00:03:02.976
“Will you be the perfect bride for the perfect
groom?”

00:03:02.976 --> 00:03:08.865
Farquaad and Duloc are rigid and inflexible,
insisting on arbitrary rules and ideals, rather

00:03:08.865 --> 00:03:11.415
than adapting to the situation at hand.

00:03:11.415 --> 00:03:16.145
Many of the film’s best jokes work by drawing
attention to the vast gulf between their delusions

00:03:16.145 --> 00:03:17.466
and their reality:

00:03:17.466 --> 00:03:21.726
Shrek thus serves as a neat illustration of
a theory of humor developed by Henri Bergson

00:03:21.726 --> 00:03:24.876
in his 1900 essay collection, Laughter.

00:03:24.876 --> 00:03:30.436
Bergson argues that “The laughable element
[...] consists of a certain MECHANICAL INELASTICITY,

00:03:30.436 --> 00:03:35.455
just where one would expect to find the wide-awake
adaptability and the living pliableness of

00:03:35.455 --> 00:03:36.455
a human being.”

00:03:36.455 --> 00:03:41.336
In other words, continuing to move as a result
of momentum, or continuing to act out a preconceived

00:03:41.336 --> 00:03:43.485
notion of how the world works.

00:03:43.485 --> 00:03:47.546
This covers everything from slipping on a
surprise banana peel to failing to adapt to

00:03:47.546 --> 00:03:54.605
changing social circumstances.

00:03:54.605 --> 00:03:55.795
Enter Shrek.

00:03:55.795 --> 00:04:02.156
Shrek is everything Farquaad is not: filthy,
smelly, and pragmatic, rather than idealistic.

00:04:02.156 --> 00:04:07.305
These traits make him the ideal comic foil
to a man with his head firmly up his own ass—and

00:04:07.305 --> 00:04:09.385
to the society he has created.

00:04:09.385 --> 00:04:13.536
Shrek is the ugly green reality check Duloc
desperately needs.

00:04:13.536 --> 00:04:18.506
Whenever he encounters an unnecessarily complex
system or highfalutin attitude, his blunt

00:04:18.506 --> 00:04:21.286
approach to life brings it crashing down.

00:04:21.286 --> 00:04:26.085
Where there is a stifling order, he deflates
it with humor, or creative disorder:

00:04:26.085 --> 00:04:28.436
“I’m an agent of chaos”

00:04:28.436 --> 00:04:33.026
To kick the movie off, Shrek literally wipes
his ass with a book of fairy tales:

00:04:33.026 --> 00:04:34.615
“Oh Lordy!”

00:04:34.615 --> 00:04:39.776
Later, he blatantly ignores social norms which
the human citizens of Duloc follow.

00:04:39.776 --> 00:04:42.536
even when they believe their lives are in
immediate danger:

00:04:42.536 --> 00:04:47.415
His no-nonsense approach to Fiona’s rescue
highlights the obvious flaws in the princess’s

00:04:47.415 --> 00:04:50.846
(and, by proxy, the audience’s) understanding
of heroism:

00:04:50.846 --> 00:04:56.006
“You’re meant to charge in, sword drawn,
banner flying—that’s what all the other

00:04:56.006 --> 00:04:57.006
knights did!”

00:04:57.006 --> 00:04:58.526
“Yeah, right before they burst into flame!”

00:04:58.526 --> 00:05:03.205
Even his speech patterns undercut the pompous
and fake “medieval” dialogue used by characters

00:05:03.205 --> 00:05:05.846
caught up in their own delusional stories.

00:05:05.846 --> 00:05:07.705
“Wait, sir knight!

00:05:07.705 --> 00:05:09.115
This beeth our first meeting.

00:05:09.115 --> 00:05:13.555
Should it not be a wonderful, romantic moment?”

00:05:13.555 --> 00:05:14.555
“Yeah.

00:05:14.555 --> 00:05:16.776
Sorry lady, there’s no time.”

00:05:16.776 --> 00:05:21.526
Just by being himself, Shrek points out the
absurdities of people like Farquaad and places

00:05:21.526 --> 00:05:26.496
like Duloc—he simplifies and loosens up
things that have gotten over-complicated and

00:05:26.496 --> 00:05:27.496
inflexible.

00:05:27.496 --> 00:05:31.186
At the same time, he complicates ideas the
rest of his world takes for granted.

00:05:31.186 --> 00:05:35.615
Most importantly, in standing up for himself
and ogres in general, he calls into question

00:05:35.615 --> 00:05:40.246
the idea that there can be simple, binary
divisions between “good” and “bad”;

00:05:40.246 --> 00:05:43.135
beauty and ugliness; perfection and imperfection.

00:05:43.135 --> 00:05:47.365
He explains this to Donkey in one of the film’s
most iconic scenes:

00:05:47.365 --> 00:05:51.305
“Onions have layers; ogres have layers.

00:05:51.305 --> 00:05:53.575
Onions have layers - you get it!

00:05:53.575 --> 00:05:55.075
We both have layers”

00:05:55.075 --> 00:05:56.075
and later:

00:05:56.075 --> 00:05:58.096
“Man they ain’t nothing but a bunch of
little dots.”

00:05:58.096 --> 00:06:01.776
“You know, Donkey, sometimes things are
more than they appear.”

00:06:01.776 --> 00:06:06.626
And in spite of Shrek’s sarcasm—and for
all the film’s reputation as a smart-alecky,

00:06:06.626 --> 00:06:11.895
postmodern irony-fest—the film revolves
around a remarkably hopeful and earnest central

00:06:11.895 --> 00:06:17.135
idea: that real life, in all its stench and
ugliness, is way more fun and full of love

00:06:17.135 --> 00:06:22.365
than the fantasies we dream up for ourselves—and
that enjoying its imperfections is the key

00:06:22.365 --> 00:06:23.755
to fulfillment.

00:06:23.755 --> 00:06:27.816
In the film’s final moments, the reality
of the friendship and love between Shrek and

00:06:27.816 --> 00:06:33.555
his band of misfits overcomes the fantasy
of a tyrant (and turns him into dragon chow):

00:06:33.555 --> 00:06:36.425
“I will have order!

00:06:36.425 --> 00:06:39.286
I will have perfection!

00:06:39.286 --> 00:06:41.436
I will have—AHHH!”

00:06:41.436 --> 00:06:46.906
Ultimately, these unorthodox but real relationships
break Shrek out of his own self-involved fantasies

00:06:46.906 --> 00:06:48.225
of contented solitude:

00:06:48.225 --> 00:06:50.485
“They judge me before they even know me...

00:06:50.485 --> 00:06:53.555
That’s why I’m better off alone.”

00:06:53.555 --> 00:06:58.785
Shrek takes a decisive step towards happiness—”ugly
ever after,” in the movie’s language—when

00:06:58.785 --> 00:07:03.635
he finally accepts his developing friendship
with Donkey and his feelings for Fiona.

00:07:03.635 --> 00:07:06.026
So, the first Shrek movie: Deep!

00:07:06.026 --> 00:07:10.006
And score for Jeffrey Katzenberg’s upstart
Dreamworks studio sticking it to the Disney

00:07:10.006 --> 00:07:11.006
machine.

00:07:11.006 --> 00:07:12.006
Sorry, Michael Eisner.

00:07:12.006 --> 00:07:13.006
“It’s okay.

00:07:13.006 --> 00:07:14.165
It’s okay.”

00:07:14.165 --> 00:07:17.475
Shrek 2 builds on the foundation Shrek lays
down.

00:07:17.475 --> 00:07:21.315
Like the first film, the sequel gets a lot
of its laughs by dragging pompous figures

00:07:21.315 --> 00:07:24.455
like Prince Charming through the mess of the
physical world.

00:07:24.455 --> 00:07:29.515
“He will rue the very day he stole my kingdom
from me!”

00:07:29.515 --> 00:07:33.716
Also like Shrek, Shrek 2 takes a dim view
of government and the politicians who run

00:07:33.716 --> 00:07:34.716
it.

00:07:34.716 --> 00:07:38.286
While from the outside he appears to be a
decent enough king, Fiona’s father Harold

00:07:38.286 --> 00:07:42.495
is ultimately revealed to be in debt to, and
under the control of, the Fairy Godmother—in

00:07:42.495 --> 00:07:45.536
other words, he is in the pocket of big business.

00:07:45.536 --> 00:07:50.786
Indeed, by turning the Fairy Godmother into
a literal manufacturer of happy endings,

00:07:50.786 --> 00:07:55.316
Shrek 2 raises interesting questions about
one of the first film’s guiding ideas.

00:07:55.316 --> 00:07:59.716
Specifically, in a world where the strongest
of human emotions are sold by the gallon,

00:07:59.716 --> 00:08:04.146
what is this “true love” that the characters
have been talking about all this time?

00:08:04.146 --> 00:08:05.396
Is it even real?

00:08:05.396 --> 00:08:07.716
“You can’t force someone to fall in love.”

00:08:07.716 --> 00:08:09.505
“Oh I beg to differ!

00:08:09.505 --> 00:08:11.666
I do it all the time.”

00:08:11.666 --> 00:08:16.305
To break it down even further: the Fairy Godmother,
maker of happy endings and fake emotions,

00:08:16.305 --> 00:08:19.065
isn’t queen bitch of just any old kingdom.

00:08:19.065 --> 00:08:23.706
She secretly wields power over a town that
is explicitly designed to lampoon that place

00:08:23.706 --> 00:08:29.337
that brews up a lot a piping-hot majority
of our own cultural BS: Beverly Hills, and

00:08:29.337 --> 00:08:30.766
nearby Hollywood.

00:08:30.766 --> 00:08:33.776
From the letters on a hill mimicking the iconic
Hollywood sign

00:08:33.776 --> 00:08:35.836
to the storefronts and movie posters,

00:08:35.836 --> 00:08:40.096
to the red carpet that greets Shrek and Fionna
as they step out of their horse-drawn limousine,

00:08:40.096 --> 00:08:45.586
Far, Far Away is full of visual cues linking
it to a town with a worldwide reputation for

00:08:45.586 --> 00:08:50.735
superficiality, run by an industry whose main
output is fictional stories meant to stir

00:08:50.735 --> 00:08:51.925
up emotion.

00:08:51.925 --> 00:08:55.815
By setting its story in such a place, the
film draws attention to the roles played by

00:08:55.815 --> 00:09:00.266
major Hollywood studios—and the system in
which they operate—in creating the out-of-touch

00:09:00.266 --> 00:09:05.486
fantasies about love, heroism, and perfection
that inspired the series in the first place.

00:09:05.486 --> 00:09:07.386
Adorno, eat your heart out.

00:09:07.386 --> 00:09:11.206
Ultimately, the film answers its questions
about “true” love in much the same way

00:09:11.206 --> 00:09:15.026
Shrek did, while raising the stakes like a
good sequel should.

00:09:15.026 --> 00:09:16.266
Let’s take a look:

00:09:16.266 --> 00:09:21.336
Like Farquaad before him, Harold is possessed
by the need to project an image of perfection—to

00:09:21.336 --> 00:09:25.876
the point where he sold away his daughter’s
future to transform himself from a frog into

00:09:25.876 --> 00:09:26.876
a human.

00:09:26.876 --> 00:09:31.666
Unlike Farquaad, Harold eventually redeems
himself by saving Fionna from the Fairy Godmother’s

00:09:31.666 --> 00:09:37.066
love potion, accepting his unusual son-in-law
for who he is, and finally dropping his human

00:09:37.066 --> 00:09:38.066
disguise:

00:09:38.066 --> 00:09:41.906
“You’re more that man today than you ever
were.

00:09:41.906 --> 00:09:43.625
Warts and all.”

00:09:43.625 --> 00:09:48.005
Then, as the clock strikes midnight, Fionna
deliberately waits for the beauty potion to

00:09:48.005 --> 00:09:51.675
wear off, so she and Shrek can revert back
to their usual selves:

00:09:51.675 --> 00:09:54.406
“I want what any princess wants.

00:09:54.406 --> 00:10:04.396
To live happily ever after—with the ogre
I married."

00:10:04.396 --> 00:10:09.526
Both of these demonstrations of love for others
involve acceptance of imperfection, but with

00:10:09.526 --> 00:10:10.526
a twist.

00:10:10.526 --> 00:10:14.836
Unlike in the first movie, both Harold and
Fionna are given opportunities (represented

00:10:14.836 --> 00:10:19.356
by the potions) to live out the rest of their
lives according to a societally sanctioned

00:10:19.356 --> 00:10:20.796
model of happiness.

00:10:20.796 --> 00:10:24.766
Harold can maintain his kingly appearance
by roofying his daughter; Fionna can have

00:10:24.766 --> 00:10:29.755
a body that fits normative standards of hotness
by sealing the beauty potion with a kiss.

00:10:29.755 --> 00:10:34.606
Imbued with this new power of choice, both
father and daughter still reject superficial

00:10:34.606 --> 00:10:38.436
perfection in favor of imperfect, but “true”
love.

00:10:38.436 --> 00:10:40.886
So Shrek 2 also gets a “Deep.”

00:10:40.886 --> 00:10:45.235
And this, unfortunately, is where things take
a turn for the stupid.

00:10:45.235 --> 00:10:50.076
Clocking in at a loooong ninety-two minutes,
Shrek the Third represents a bizarre departure

00:10:50.076 --> 00:10:53.016
from everything the series has stood for thus
far.

00:10:53.016 --> 00:10:57.556
With Harold dead and Fionna pregnant, Shrek
is terrified by the looming responsibilities

00:10:57.556 --> 00:11:01.745
of kingship and fatherhood.

00:11:01.745 --> 00:11:04.706
Yeesh.

00:11:04.706 --> 00:11:08.966
Hoping to get at least one thing off his plate,
Shrek tracks down Arthur Pendragon, Harold’s

00:11:08.966 --> 00:11:11.925
nephew and the only other viable heir to the
throne.

00:11:11.925 --> 00:11:16.176
In his absence, Prince Charming raises an
army of jealous villains to invade Far, Far

00:11:16.176 --> 00:11:19.076
Away and secure their own “happily-ever-afters.”

00:11:19.076 --> 00:11:23.636
The final showdown sees young Arthur step
into his role as king by convincing the villains

00:11:23.636 --> 00:11:26.935
that only they have the power to make themselves
happy.

00:11:26.935 --> 00:11:29.565
While the film does have a few great standalone
jokes:

00:11:29.565 --> 00:11:30.736
“Well, well, well.

00:11:30.736 --> 00:11:32.706
If it isn’t Peter Pan.”

00:11:32.706 --> 00:11:34.276
“His name’s not Peter!”

00:11:34.276 --> 00:11:35.445
“Shut it, Wendy.”

00:11:35.445 --> 00:11:39.516
It’s missing the core of what made the first
two movies in the series so powerful.

00:11:39.516 --> 00:11:41.966
Listen to Arthur’s big speech to the villains:

00:11:41.966 --> 00:11:44.976
“You’re telling me you just want to be
villains your whole lives?

00:11:44.976 --> 00:11:46.995
Didn’t you ever wish you could be something
else?

00:11:46.995 --> 00:11:52.116
If there’s something you really want, or
someone you really want to be, then the only

00:11:52.116 --> 00:11:55.505
person standing in your way is you."

00:11:55.505 --> 00:11:56.505
"Me?"

00:11:56.505 --> 00:11:59.995
Coming from this series, this is really, really
dumb.

00:11:59.995 --> 00:12:03.246
Putting aside for a moment that Arthur seems
to have lifted his dialogue straight from

00:12:03.246 --> 00:12:07.886
a YouTube workout motivation video, what’s
so off about this moment is that it completely

00:12:07.886 --> 00:12:11.226
undercuts what the series has done so far.

00:12:11.226 --> 00:12:16.056
Shrek and Shrek 2 point out the inherent problems
in black-and-white thinking: dividing people

00:12:16.056 --> 00:12:20.956
into camps of “good” and “bad,” “heroes”
and “villains,” is both unrealistic and

00:12:20.956 --> 00:12:21.956
harmful.

00:12:21.956 --> 00:12:26.855
The films culminate in main characters accepting,
and even celebrating, the inevitably flawed

00:12:26.855 --> 00:12:28.716
reality they inhabit.

00:12:28.716 --> 00:12:33.005
Shrek the Turd (you heard me) uncritically
promotes the idea of “villainy” as an

00:12:33.005 --> 00:12:37.526
objective state of being — one that villains
can escape only by aligning their behavior

00:12:37.526 --> 00:12:39.786
with what is socially acceptable.

00:12:39.786 --> 00:12:45.005
It completely sh*tcans the complex discussion
around acceptance that carries the first two

00:12:45.005 --> 00:12:50.026
films, and opts instead for warmed-over cliches
from a self-help seminar.

00:12:50.026 --> 00:12:54.676
Oh, and you get the privilege of watching
a group of teenage girls hit on a middle-aged

00:12:54.676 --> 00:12:55.676
man.

00:12:55.676 --> 00:13:00.435
“This is like totally embarrassing, but
my friend Tiffany thinkest thou vex her so

00:13:00.435 --> 00:13:04.276
soothly, and she thought perchance thou would
wanna ask her to the Homecoming Dance or something.”

00:13:04.276 --> 00:13:05.386
“Uh, excuse me?”

00:13:05.386 --> 00:13:06.386
“It’s like whatever.

00:13:06.386 --> 00:13:09.266
She’s just totally into college guys and
mythical creatures and stuff.”

00:13:09.266 --> 00:13:11.566
This movie is Dumb.

00:13:11.566 --> 00:13:14.406
Shrek Forever After doesn’t fare much better.

00:13:14.406 --> 00:13:17.596
Like Shrek the Third, it does have some stand-out
moments:

00:13:17.596 --> 00:13:21.896
Also like Shrek the Third, it completely abandons
the take on happiness and imperfection that

00:13:21.896 --> 00:13:24.876
made the first two entries in the series so
interesting.

00:13:24.876 --> 00:13:29.596
The film is a Shrek-style spin on the It’s
a Wonderful Life formula you’ll remember

00:13:29.596 --> 00:13:33.396
from: the original Jimmy Stewart production;
that Muppet Christmas movie where Kermit gets

00:13:33.396 --> 00:13:37.766
traumatized by a restaurant serving frog legs;
and many other iterations.

00:13:37.766 --> 00:13:41.916
Feeling trapped by the daily grind of responsible
parenthood, Shrek makes an impulsive deal

00:13:41.916 --> 00:13:46.185
with Rumpelstiltskin, and winds up stuck in
a world in which he was never born, and therefore

00:13:46.185 --> 00:13:49.596
never married Fionna, met his friends, or
had his children.

00:13:49.596 --> 00:13:53.745
After learning the value of what he lost,
Shrek is able to nullify his contract with

00:13:53.745 --> 00:13:58.706
Rumpel by sucking face with underground - ogre
- resistance - leader Fionna, and return to

00:13:58.706 --> 00:13:59.706
his family.

00:13:59.706 --> 00:14:03.036
All of which sounds just fine and dandy, until
you look at the fine print.

00:14:03.036 --> 00:14:04.476
“His fine print is crafty.”

00:14:04.476 --> 00:14:07.706
Listen to the last thing Fionna says to Shrek
before he makes his deal:

00:14:07.706 --> 00:14:10.445
"You have three beautiful children.

00:14:10.445 --> 00:14:13.396
A wife who loves you.

00:14:13.396 --> 00:14:14.446
Friends who adore you.

00:14:14.446 --> 00:14:16.795
You have everything.”

00:14:16.795 --> 00:14:21.866
Now, membership in a nuclear family and the
support of a good network may be great things

00:14:21.866 --> 00:14:26.676
to have—but the Fionna who gorged on Weed
Rat Rotisserie Style, challenged her husband

00:14:26.676 --> 00:14:31.185
to a fart contest in a mud bath, and chose
to remain an ogre, rather than change the

00:14:31.185 --> 00:14:36.076
person she loved would not say that these
conventional markers of success and happiness

00:14:36.076 --> 00:14:37.466
are “everything.”

00:14:37.466 --> 00:14:41.156
The film puts Shrek in a position similar
to that of the villains at the end of Shrek

00:14:41.156 --> 00:14:42.156
the Third.

00:14:42.156 --> 00:14:46.245
Above and beyond attending to the responsibilities
of fatherhood, he is being asked to abandon

00:14:46.245 --> 00:14:50.586
key parts of an identity that brings him joy
and a sense of purpose.

00:14:50.586 --> 00:14:55.355
Although people inevitably, and rightfully,
sacrifice a lot for their families, it’s

00:14:55.355 --> 00:14:57.526
romanticized in a way reminiscent of Duloc.

00:14:57.526 --> 00:15:02.797
He is expected to insert himself into an accepted
cultural narrative of the “perfect” life

00:15:02.797 --> 00:15:09.036
is — in other words, to buy completely into
the story manufactured in Far, Far Away: a

00:15:09.036 --> 00:15:13.736
fairy tale that the series spent two remarkable
movies trying to dismantle.

00:15:13.736 --> 00:15:18.386
Shrek Forever After is Dumb—and no amount
of bestiarii-Gingy can make it Deep.

00:15:18.386 --> 00:15:19.875
So it looks like we have a tie.

00:15:19.875 --> 00:15:23.105
While there’s no getting around the fact
that the series lost a lot of its depth as

00:15:23.105 --> 00:15:28.466
time went on, the later films still offer
their fair share of weird, clever jokes, not

00:15:28.466 --> 00:15:30.836
all of them grounded in pop-culture references:

00:15:30.836 --> 00:15:33.906
“It's time to pay the piper.

00:15:33.906 --> 00:15:38.636
Griselda seriously, it's time to pay the piper!

00:15:38.636 --> 00:15:40.086
Now go get my check book!

00:15:40.086 --> 00:15:41.086
Go, move, get out!

00:15:41.086 --> 00:15:44.986
And the films’ best work is often more complex
than the series as a whole gets credit for.

00:15:44.986 --> 00:15:47.246
"Well maybe you're right princess!"

00:15:47.246 --> 00:15:48.266
What do you think, Wisecrack?

00:15:48.266 --> 00:15:51.706
Are these films deeper or dumber than we’ve
determined here today?

00:15:51.706 --> 00:15:53.816
Did you spot something we missed?

00:15:53.816 --> 00:15:58.816
Is “Shrek in the Swamp Karaoke Dance Party”
canon, and could Lord Farquaad still be alive

00:15:58.816 --> 00:16:00.366
inside Dragon’s cavernous stomach?

